==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sent-|id=perceive}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|cōnsēnsus||agreement, accordance, unanimity}}, from {{m|la|cōnsentiō||feel together; agree}}; see {{m|en|consent}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kənˈsɛnsəs/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-PerfectSoundWhatever-consensus.wav|a=CA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Wodencafe-consensus.wav|a=US}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To seek consensus; to hold discussions with the aim of reaching mutual agreement.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1975|author=United States Bureau of the Census|title=The Census Bureau|page=168
|passage=I think we are<!--the word "are" is absent from some copies of the text but present in others--> a strongly '''consensused''' society. There was a consensus during the 1950's, the Eisenhower years, in our society. Then in the 1960's came a period of division.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=United States House Committee on Science, Space, and Technology. Subcommittee on Environment|title=The Science of Wetland Definition and Delineation: Hearing Before the Subcommittee on Environment of the Committee on Science, Space, and Technology, U.S. House of Representatives, One Hundred Second Congress, First Session, November 12, 1991|page=185
|passage=None of this '''consensusing''' was done with the Manual. There were no national workshops, forums, etc.}}

